**What changed:** In v3.2.1, `HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration` is now declared to run before `DataSourceTransactionManagerAutoConfiguration`. Its class-level annotation changed from ordering after `DataSourceAutoConfiguration` to `@AutoConfiguration(after = { DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class, TransactionManagerCustomizationAutoConfiguration.class }, before = { TransactionAutoConfiguration.class, DataSourceTransactionManagerAutoConfiguration.class })` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.2.1/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-autoconfigure/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/autoconfigure/orm/jpa/HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration.java). Previously (3.2.0), `DataSourceTransactionManagerAutoConfiguration` could run first and register its `DataSourceTransactionManager`; both it and `JpaBaseConfiguration.transactionManager(...)` use `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(TransactionManager.class)`, so the Hibernate `JpaTransactionManager` bean could be skipped even when Hibernate was on the classpath (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38880, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38861). Listed as a bug fix in the v3.2.1 release (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.2.1).

**Affects:** applications on Hibernate/JPA with auto-configured transactions where the `TransactionManager` bean type was previously the auto-configured `DataSourceTransactionManager`.

**Action:** required: re-verify that the auto-configured `TransactionManager` is now a `JpaTransactionManager` rather than a `DataSourceTransactionManager`, and that any custom `TransactionManager` beans with `@ConditionalOnMissingBean(TransactionManager.class)` still resolve as expected.

**Before/after:**
```java
// After (v3.2.1): HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration.java
@AutoConfiguration(
        after = { DataSourceAutoConfiguration.class, TransactionManagerCustomizationAutoConfiguration.class },
        before = { TransactionAutoConfiguration.class, DataSourceTransactionManagerAutoConfiguration.class })
@ConditionalOnClass({ LocalContainerEntityManagerFactoryBean.class, EntityManager.class, SessionImplementor.class })
@EnableConfigurationProperties(JpaProperties.class)
@Import(HibernateJpaConfiguration.class)
public class HibernateJpaAutoConfiguration { }
```

**What changed:** Spring Boot 3.2.0's PR #37905 (making `@Order` on `@Bean` methods for runners work) changed `SpringApplication::callRunners` to look up runners via `context.getBeanProvider(Runner.class)`, using the new sealed marker interface implemented by both `ApplicationRunner` and `CommandLineRunner` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/pull/37905). Because `getBeanProvider` resolves beans from ancestor bean factories, a child context started via `new SpringApplicationBuilder(childMainClass).parent((ConfigurableApplicationContext) applicationContext).bannerMode(Banner.Mode.OFF).build()` then `springApplication.run()` executed the *parent's* `CommandLineRunner`/`ApplicationRunner` beans, causing infinite recursion when the parent's runner itself creates the child context. This regression (`type: regression`, forward port of #38647 to 3.2.x as #38837) is fixed in 3.2.1, where the release notes list "Child context created with SpringApplicationBuilder runs parents runners #38837" among the bug fixes (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38647, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38837). The reporter identified the root cause as `context.getBeanProvider(Runner.class)` replacing `context.getBeansOfType(ApplicationRunner.class)` in `SpringApplication::callRunners` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38647).

**Affects:** Applications on exactly 3.2.0 that start a child context with `SpringApplicationBuilder.parent(...)` — particularly when parent runner beans exist and/or the child is created from within a parent `CommandLineRunner`/`ApplicationRunner`. On 3.2.1 the regression is fixed, so upgrading resolves it.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```java
// Executed from a parent's CommandLineRunner under 3.2.0:
// the child run() re-invoked the PARENT's runners -> infinite recursion.
SpringApplication springApplication = new SpringApplicationBuilder(childMainClass)
        .parent((ConfigurableApplicationContext) applicationContext)
        .bannerMode(Banner.Mode.OFF)
        .build();
ConfigurableApplicationContext context = springApplication.run();
```

**What changed:** Spring Boot 3.2.0 introduced a rewritten jar loader whose class `org.springframework.boot.loader.zip.ZipString` computes hash codes of ZIP entries' file names directly from `DataBlock` views of the central directory. When a file name containing a multi-byte UTF-8 character happened to straddle the end of the data block being processed, `ZipString.hash` (via `readInBuffer`) requested the missing continuation byte, which the block could not supply — it returned only the first byte of the multi-byte character repeatedly — so the loop in `ZipString.hash` never terminated and the application hung in a `RUNNABLE` state at `org.springframework.boot.loader.zip.ZipString.readInBuffer(ZipString.java:281)` / `ZipString.hash(ZipString.java:112)`, called from `ZipContent$Loader.add(ZipContent.java:445)` during very early startup of an executable jar (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38751). Issue `#38751` was labeled `type: regression`, milestoned in **3.2.1**, fixed by @philwebb, and listed under "🐞 Bug Fixes" as "Multi-byte filenames in zip files can cause an endless loop in ZipString.hash #38751" in the v3.2.1 release notes (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.2.1). Related loader hash/central-directory regressions fixed in the same release include `NegativeArraySizeException ... ZipContent$Loader` #38572, `ZipException: read CEN tables failed` #38595, and `#38660` jar launcher failing in folders with certain characters.

**Affects:** Applications packaged as an executable ("super") jar running on Spring Boot 3.2.0, where a nested jar in the archive has an entry whose name contains a multi-byte character, causing startup to hang.

**Action:** none — this is a fixed regression in the loader that ships inside the application's jar; no application code, build, or configuration changes beyond using the 3.2.1 loader are described in the sources. Applications currently hanging should re-verify startup of `java -jar` and `java -Djarmode=layertools -jar ... extract` on the new loader.

**Before/after:**
```java
// 3.2.0 hang, thread dump from issue #38751:
// java.lang.Thread.State: RUNNABLE
//   at org.springframework.boot.loader.zip.ZipString.readInBuffer(ZipString.java:281)
//   at org.springframework.boot.loader.zip.ZipString.hash(ZipString.java:112)
//   at org.springframework.boot.loader.zip.ZipContent$Loader.add(ZipContent.java:445)
//   at org.springframework.boot.loader.zip.ZipContent$Loader.loadContent(ZipContent.java:581)
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.0, custom `Converter` or `GenericConverter` beans registered in the application context were not used when binding properties to collection types (such as `List<CustomType>`), resulting in a `ConverterNotFoundException`. This occurred because the `beansConverterService` introduced in 3.2.0 lacked support for collection-based conversions (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/commit/f4e05c91c74e). Spring Boot 3.2.1 fixes this regression by restoring support for custom bind converters in collections, specifically by adding `DefaultConversionService.addCollectionConverters()` to the `beansConverterService` in `ConversionServiceDeducer` ().

**Affects:** Applications using custom `Converter` beans registered in the context to bind configuration properties to collection types like `List` or `Set` when upgrading from 3.2.0 to 3.2.1.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```java
// 3.2.0 beansConverterService creation in ConversionServiceDeducer
FormattingConversionService beansConverterService = new FormattingConversionService();
converterBeans.addTo(beansConverterService);

// 3.2.1 beansConverterService creation in ConversionServiceDeducer
FormattingConversionService beansConverterService = new FormattingConversionService();
DefaultConversionService.addCollectionConverters(beansConverterService);
converterBeans.addTo(beansConverterService);
```

**What changed:** In 3.2.0, `ReactiveUserDetailsServiceAutoConfiguration` backs off more readily (gh-35338, e.g. `@ConditionalOnMissingClass` on `ReactiveOpaqueTokenIntrospector` / `ClientRepository` brought in by `spring-boot-starter-oauth2-resource-server` / oauth2-login), so a WebFlux app can end up with no `ReactiveAuthenticationManager` and no `ReactiveUserDetailsService`; `ReactiveSecurityAutoConfiguration` then withheld `@EnableWebFluxSecurity`, and with resource-server starters present the context failed to start with `IllegalArgumentException: authenticationManager cannot be null` from `AuthenticationWebFilter` (gh-38713, gh-37504). Fixed in 3.2.1 (commit afad358, "Align reactive web security more closely with servlet web security"): `ReactiveSecurityAutoConfiguration` now auto-configures a `denyAllAuthenticationManager` `ReactiveAuthenticationManager` bean (returns `Mono.error(new UsernameNotFoundException(authentication.getName()))`) when there is no `ReactiveAuthenticationManager`, `ReactiveUserDetailsService`, or `SecurityWebFilterChain`, and `@EnableWebFluxSecurity` (`EnableWebFluxSecurityConfiguration`, conditional on missing `WebFilterChainProxy`) is then applied in the reactive case as it is in servlet. The reactive OAuth2 client and resource-server auto-configurations (`ReactiveOAuth2ClientConfigurations`, `ReactiveOAuth2ResourceServerJwkConfiguration`, `ReactiveOAuth2ResourceServerOpaqueTokenConfiguration`) no longer declare their own `@EnableWebFluxSecurity` configuration, relying on `ReactiveSecurityAutoConfiguration` instead (, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38713).

**Affects:** Reactive (`spring-boot-starter-webflux`) applications using `spring-boot-starter-security` with oauth2-resource-server or oauth2-client on the classpath, and any app that excluded `ReactiveUserDetailsServiceAutoConfiguration`; on 3.2.1 this is a bug fix, not a required change.

**Action:** none (upgrading to 3.2.1 restores startup; re-verify that with no `ReactiveAuthenticationManager`/`ReactiveUserDetailsService`, unauthenticated requests now get 401/deny-all via `denyAllAuthenticationManager` rather than the context backing off or failing).

**Before/after:**
```java
// 3.2.1 ReactiveSecurityAutoConfiguration (new)
@Bean
@ConditionalOnMissingBean({ ReactiveAuthenticationManager.class, ReactiveUserDetailsService.class,
        SecurityWebFilterChain.class })
ReactiveAuthenticationManager denyAllAuthenticationManager() {
    return (authentication) -> Mono.error(new UsernameNotFoundException(authentication.getName()));
}

@Configuration(proxyBeanMethods = false)
@ConditionalOnMissingBean(WebFilterChainProxy.class)
@EnableWebFluxSecurity
static class EnableWebFluxSecurityConfiguration {
}
```

**What changed:** The issue #38661 (forward-port of #38398) reports that `spring-boot-docker-compose` silently skips the startup phase if one or more services are already running, leading to incomplete container startup for remaining services. The reported cause is in `org.springframework.boot.docker.compose.lifecycle.DockerComposeLifecycleManager#start`, which only calls `docker compose up` when `runningServices.isEmpty()` is true. The provided documentation confirms the intended behavior: "If the Docker Compose services are already running when starting the application, Spring Boot will only create the service connection beans for each supported container. It will not call `docker compose up` again" ().

**Affects:** Applications using `spring-boot-docker-compose` that include a `compose.yml` file with multiple services, particularly if some containers are started manually or externally before the Spring Boot application starts.

**Action:** unclear from the sources

**Before/after:**
```java
// Condition in DockerComposeLifecycleManager#start described in the issue
// Startup is skipped if ANY service is already running:
if (lifecycleManagement.shouldStart() && runningServices.isEmpty()){
   // call docker compose up
}
```

**What changed:** Spring Boot 3.2.0 set the `ObservationRegistry` only on the auto-configured `JmsTemplate` (via #37388), so `@JmsListener` annotated methods were not observed by default. Issue #38613 (milestone 3.2.1, fixed by commit `0321a8a`) added the missing counter-part: the `ObservationRegistry` is now also set on the auto-configured `JmsListenerContainerFactory`, by configuring `DefaultJmsListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer` with the registry — the configurer gained a `setObservationRegistry(io.micrometer.observation.ObservationRegistry observationRegistry)` method that is used by `configure(DefaultJmsListenerContainerFactory factory, ConnectionFactory connectionFactory)` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38613, ).

**Affects:** Applications using Spring Boot JMS auto-configuration (`JmsAutoConfiguration` / auto-configured `jmsListenerContainerFactory`) together with an auto-configured `ObservationRegistry` (Micrometer observation on the classpath) and `@JmsListener` methods.

**Action:** none — this is a bug fix delivered in the patch release; no code, config or dependency change is required. Re-verify that JMS listener metrics/traces now appear (and expect additional observation-related metrics/spans that were absent in 3.2.0), and that applications which manually configured `DefaultJmsListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer` still get the registry they intend.

**Before/after:**
```java
// new public API on the auto-configuration configurer (3.2.1+)
public class DefaultJmsListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer {
    public DefaultJmsListenerContainerFactoryConfigurer() {}

    public void setObservationRegistry(io.micrometer.observation.ObservationRegistry observationRegistry) { ... }

    public void configure(DefaultJmsListenerContainerFactory factory, ConnectionFactory connectionFactory) { ... }
}
```

Based on the fetched GitHub issue and release notes for Spring Boot 3.2.1:

**What changed:** When using `RestTemplateBuilder` to build a `RestTemplate` backed by Apache HttpClient 5 (`HttpComponentsClientHttpRequestFactory`), system-provided SSL certificates (configured via Java system properties) are now automatically picked up and used. Previously, while `new RestTemplate()` (using `SimpleClientHttpRequestFactory`) and parts of `RestTemplateBuilder` respected system SSL properties, the Apache HttpClient integration in `RestTemplateBuilder` did not consistently apply them unless explicitly configured. This fix aligns `RestTemplateBuilder`'s behavior with `new RestTemplate()` by ensuring the underlying `HttpClientBuilder` is configured to use system properties for SSL (`useSystemProperties()` effectively) as part of the auto-configuration defaults (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38600, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.2.1).

**Affects:** Applications using `RestTemplateBuilder` to create `RestTemplate` instances when Apache HttpClient 5 is on the classpath and the application relies on system-level SSL configuration (e.g., Cloud Foundry security providers or JVM system properties for truststores) without explicitly configuring SSL bundles or custom `HttpClient` beans.

**Action:** none. The change is a bug fix restoring expected behavior. Applications relying on the previous (broken) behavior where system SSL certs were ignored by `RestTemplateBuilder` with Apache HttpClient might see changed SSL handshake behavior, but generally no code change is required to adopt the fix. If custom `HttpClient` beans were injected via `RestTemplateCustomizer` to manually enable system properties, verify they still work as intended, but standard `RestTemplateBuilder` usage should now correctly use system SSL settings.

**Before/after:**
```java
// Before: In environments providing SSL certs via system properties,
// this might fail to trust system certs if Apache HttpClient was used via auto-config.
RestTemplate restTemplate = restTemplateBuilder.build();

// After: System SSL certificates are now correctly picked up by default.
// No code change required for this specific fix.
RestTemplate restTemplate = restTemplateBuilder.build();
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.0 the new nested jar support introduced a regression where nested jar URLs could not be split and reassembled, causing errors for projects that parse these URLs (e.g., to list files inside nested jars via `java.nio.file.FileSystems.newFileSystem`). The returned `FileSystem` was of type `org.springframework.boot.loader.nio.file.NestedFileSystem`. This regression was fixed in 3.2.1, restoring the expected behavior where URLs like `jar:nested:...!/BOOT-INF/lib/...!/` can be correctly processed (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38592, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.2.1)

**Affects:** Applications using nested jar techniques that programmatically open `JarURLConnection` to nested resources and attempt to create a `FileSystem` to list files within them, such as JobRunr reading migrations from the classpath (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38592)

**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```java
var url = ((JarURLConnection)H2StorageProvider.class.getResource("migrations").openConnection()).getJarFileURL();
FileSystem fileSystem = FileSystems.newFileSystem(url.toURI(), Collections.emptyMap());
Path path = fileSystem.getPath("BOOT-INF/lib/jobrunr-6.3.3.jar!/org/jobrunr/storage/sql/common/migrations/");
boolean exists = Files.exists(path);
List<Path> list = Files.list(path).collect(Collectors.toList());
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.0, `org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.server.Session.getCookie()` became binary-incompatible with earlier versions: code compiled against 3.1.x that calls `Session.getCookie()` expecting a return type of `org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.server.Session$Cookie` fails at runtime on 3.2.0 with `java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: 'org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.server.Session$Cookie org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.server.Session.getCookie()'` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38589). A typical trigger is `serverProperties.getServlet().getSession().getCookie().getName()` on `ServerProperties` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38589). Spring Boot 3.2.1 lists this as a fixed regression: "NoSuchMethodError can be thrown from Session.getCookie() due to binary incompatibilty #38589" under Bug Fixes in the v3.2.1 release notes (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.2.1).

**Affects:** Applications or libraries compiled against Spring Boot ≤ 3.1.x that call `Session.getCookie()` (e.g., via `ServerProperties`) but are run against Spring Boot 3.2.0 only; code compiled against 3.2.x is unaffected.

**Action:** required: re-verify that no `NoSuchMethodError` is thrown from `Session.getCookie()` at runtime; recompiling the affected code against the 3.2.x line also resolves the mismatch. No configuration or dependency-coordinate change is documented beyond moving off the 3.2.0 artifacts.

**Before/after:**
```java
// Compiled against Spring Boot 3.1.6, run against 3.2.0 -> NoSuchMethodError:
final String sessionCookieName = Optional.ofNullable(
        serverProperties.getServlet().getSession().getCookie().getName())
    .orElse("SESSION");
// Caused by: java.lang.NoSuchMethodError: 'org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.server.Session$Cookie
// org.springframework.boot.web.servlet.server.Session.getCookie()'
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.0, the "default" `Tracer` previously available in sliced tests was removed, causing failures if no tracer implementation was on the classpath. This was addressed in Spring Boot 3.2.1 as a regression fix (issue #38568). Two key changes restore the behavior: 1) The `@AutoConfigureObservability` annotation can now be applied to sliced tests (e.g., `@WebMvcTest`). If applied, it auto-configures an in-memory `MeterRegistry`, a no-op `Tracer`, and an `ObservationRegistry` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/v3.2.1/spring-boot-project/spring-boot-test-autoconfigure/src/main/java/org/springframework/boot/test/autoconfigure/actuate/observability/AutoConfigureObservability.java, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38568). 2) If Micrometer Tracing is on the classpath but no specific implementation (like Brave or OpenTelemetry) supplies a `Tracer`, a no-op `Tracer` is now auto-configured for symmetry with `MeterRegistry` and `ObservationRegistry` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38568).

**Affects:** Applications upgrading to 3.2.1 that rely on auto-configured `Tracer` beans in sliced tests or have Micrometer Tracing on the classpath without a specific bridge implementation.

**Action:** required: Annotate sliced tests that require observability beans with `@AutoConfigureObservability`. Alternatively, ensure a tracer implementation (e.g., `micrometer-tracing-bridge-otel`) is on the classpath, or verify that the new auto-configuration of a no-op `Tracer` satisfies the application's requirements.

**Before/after:**
```java
// Before (3.2.0): Tracer missing in sliced tests unless @SpringBootTest used
@WebMvcTest
class MyControllerTest { ... }

// After (3.2.1): Opt-in to observability auto-configuration in sliced tests
@AutoConfigureObservability
@WebMvcTest
class MyControllerTest { ... }
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.0, `bindOnInit=false` was set only on the main connector (in `TomcatServletWebServerFactory` and `TomcatReactiveWebServerFactory`), so additional connectors added via `addAdditionalTomcatConnectors` bound their sockets at init and never released them on `TomcatWebServer.stop()`, causing `java.net.BindException: Address already in use` when a second test context reuses the fixed port (github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38564, github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38554). The 3.2.1 fix moved bind-on-init disabling into `TomcatWebServer.initialize()` via a new `disableBindOnInit()` helper that sets `connector.setProperty("bindOnInit", "false")` on every connector unless `bindOnInit` was explicitly set (github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/commit/8de81cb06e7039e95989f6035565f810edeaba4b).

**Affects:** Applications using embedded Tomcat that add extra connectors (e.g. via `TomcatServletWebServerFactory.addAdditionalTomcatConnectors(Connector[])` or a separate management port) with fixed ports, especially sequential `@SpringBootTest` runs; fixed by upgrading to 3.2.1, no application change required.

**Action:** none
**Before/after:**
```java
// TomcatServletWebServerFactory.customizeConnector (3.2.0) — removed in 3.2.1
// Don't bind to the socket prematurely if ApplicationContext is slow to start
connector.setProperty("bindOnInit", "false");

// TomcatWebServer.initialize() (3.2.1) — applies to ALL connectors
private void disableBindOnInit() {
    doWithConnectors((service, connectors) -> {
        for (Connector connector : connectors) {
            Object bindOnInit = connector.getProperty("bindOnInit");
            if (bindOnInit == null) {
                connector.setProperty("bindOnInit", "false");
            }
        }
    });
}
```

**What changed:** In 3.2.0, the actuator child management context was started unconditionally by `ChildManagementContextInitializer.start()`, even when the parent application context was a mock/no-server context created by `@SpringBootTest(webEnvironment = MOCK)`. This caused a separate management web server to bind the configured management port, so running tests sequentially failed with `java.net.BindException: Address already in use: bind`. 3.2.1 (commit `75a89556599c7a9f9f84d1cbdff5058c8bb14aff`) added a guard so `start()` returns early unless `parentContext instanceof WebServerApplicationContext`, i.e. the management context only starts when the parent actually has a web server (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/commit/75a89556599c7a9f9f84d1cbdff5058c8bb14aff.patch, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.2.1)

**Affects:** Applications using spring-boot-starter-actuator that set `management.server.port` (a separate management port) and run `@SpringBootTest` tests with `webEnvironment = MOCK` (or none) that are executed sequentially in the same build (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38554)

**Action:** none (the binding regression is fixed by the 3.2.1 patch; no code or configuration change required). A documented workaround for users who cannot upgrade to 3.2.1 is to set the actuator port equal to the regular server port (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38554?timeline_page=1)

**Before/after:**
```java
// ChildManagementContextInitializer.start() — after the 3.2.1 fix
@Override
public void start() {
    if (!(this.parentContext instanceof WebServerApplicationContext)) {
        return;
    }
    if (this.managementContext == null) {
        ConfigurableApplicationContext managementContext = createManagementContext();
        registerBeans(managementContext);
        ...
    }
    ...
}
```

**What changed:** In Spring Boot 3.2.0, setting `spring.task.scheduling.shutdown.await-termination-period` (or `spring.task.execution.shutdown.await-termination-period`) did not propagate to the underlying `SimpleAsyncTaskScheduler` / `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` graceful-termination timeout, so the scheduler/executor kept its default `taskTerminationTimeout` and shutdown did not honor the configured period (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38530, https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/38528). In 3.2.1 the auto-configuration applies the value: `TaskSchedulingConfigurations` now calls `builder.taskTerminationTimeout(shutdown.getAwaitTerminationPeriod())` when `shutdown.isAwaitTermination()` is true, and `SimpleAsyncTaskSchedulerBuilder` gained `public SimpleAsyncTaskSchedulerBuilder taskTerminationTimeout(Duration taskTerminationTimeout)` which maps via `map.from(this.taskTerminationTimeout).as(Duration::toMillis).to(taskScheduler::setTaskTerminationTimeout)` (). The analogous fix for `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor`/`SimpleAsyncTaskExecutorBuilder` is tracked by #38528 (milestone 3.2.1).

**Affects:** Applications using the auto-configured `SimpleAsyncTaskScheduler` / `SimpleAsyncTaskExecutor` (e.g. with virtual threads enabled) that set `spring.task.scheduling.shutdown.await-termination` plus `spring.task.scheduling.shutdown.await-termination-period`, or the matching `spring.task.execution.shutdown.*` properties.

**Action:** none — code and configuration are unchanged; re-verify graceful shutdown now actually waits for the configured `await-termination-period` (previously ignored for these simple executors). If you build your own builder, you may now use `SimpleAsyncTaskSchedulerBuilder.taskTerminationTimeout(Duration)`.

**Before/after:**
```java
// 3.2.1: TaskSchedulingConfigurations applies the period to the scheduler
TaskSchedulingProperties.Shutdown shutdown = this.properties.getShutdown();
if (shutdown.isAwaitTermination()) {
    builder = builder.taskTerminationTimeout(shutdown.getAwaitTerminationPeriod());
}
```

**What changed:** This is a bug fix, not an intentional behavior change: GitHub issue #33731, "Controller level exceptions not getting populated in HTTP server requests metrics" (filed Jan 2023, closed, milestone 3.2.1, assignee bclozel), was fixed in Spring Boot 3.2.1 and is listed under "🐞 Bug Fixes — Controller level exceptions not getting populated in HTTP server requests metrics #33731" in the v3.2.1 release notes (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/releases/tag/v3.2.1). Before 3.2.1, exceptions handled by a controller-level `@ExceptionHandler` (or `@ControllerAdvice`) were not recorded on the observation context, so the `exception` tag was absent from `http.server.requests` (visible at `/actuator/metrics/http.server.requests` under `availableTags -> exception`); only exceptions handled internally by Spring MVC appeared. The documented workaround before the fix, shown in the issue, was to look up the context manually in the handler: `ServerHttpObservationFilter.findObservationContext(request).ifPresent(context -> ...)` (https://github.com/spring-projects/spring-boot/issues/33731).

**Affects:** Servlet MVC applications that use `@ExceptionHandler` methods to handle exceptions locally and that rely on the `exception` tag of the `http.server.requests` metric (actuator/micrometer-observation).

**Action:** none — upgrading restores correct population of the `exception` tag; only re-verify that dashboards/alerts keyed on `http.server.requests` `exception` now receive extra series (exceptions previously tagged `None` may now carry the exception class name).

**Before/after:**
```java
@Controller
public class MyController {

	@ExceptionHandler(CustomException.class)
	String handleCustomException(HttpServletRequest request, CustomException ex) {
		ServerHttpObservationFilter.findObservationContext(request)
				.ifPresent(context -> ...
```
